.page ls 1 list directory contents

# SYNOPSIS
*ls* [*-aAdFhilpRrSt1*] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`ls` lists the files named on the command line and the contents of the directories named on it. Without an argument it lists the current directory. Names that start with a dot are left out unless `-a` or `-A` is given.

Operands that are not directories are listed first, then each directory in turn. When more than one operand is given, or with `-R`, every directory's listing is introduced by its name and a colon.

On a terminal the names are arranged in columns (filled top to bottom) and coloured by kind: directories bold blue, symbolic links bold cyan, devices bold yellow, named pipes yellow, executable files bold green. When standard output is not a terminal the names come one per line and uncoloured, which is what pipelines want.

Entries are sorted by name; `-t` and `-S` change the key and `-r` reverses the result.

# OPTIONS
@ *-a*
    Show every entry, including `.` and `..` and names that start with a dot.
@ *-A*
    Like `-a`, but leave out `.` and `..`.
@ *-d*
    List a directory operand itself instead of its contents.
@ *-F*
    Append a mark to each name: `/` for a directory, `*` for an executable file, `@` for a symbolic link, `|` for a named pipe.
@ *-h*
    With `-l`, print sizes in human readable form (1.4M instead of 1468006).
@ *-i*
    Print the inode number in front of each name.
@ *-l*
    Long format: type and permissions, link count, owner, group, size, modification time and name. A symbolic link is followed by `->` and its target. A `total` line gives the size of the directory's entries in KiB.
@ *-p*
    Append `/` to directory names.
@ *-R*
    List subdirectories recursively.
@ *-r*
    Reverse the sort order.
@ *-S*
    Sort by size, largest first.
@ *-t*
    Sort by modification time, newest first.
@ *-1*
    One entry per line, even on a terminal.
@ *-C*
    Accepted for compatibility; columns are already the default on a terminal.

Options may be combined (`ls -laR`). A lone `--` ends the options.

# EXAMPLES
    ls -l /Hakurei/etc
    ls -lh /bin | sort -k5 -h
    ls -ltr /BambooForest
    ls -d /HumanVillage/*

# NOTES
Times are shown in UTC; Gensokyo keeps its own time zone. Files modified within the last 180 days show the time of day, older ones show the year.

# EXIT STATUS
`ls` exits 0 when everything was listed, 1 when a directory could not be read, and 2 when an operand does not exist or an option is not recognised.

# SEE ALSO
find(1), stat(1), cat(1), gensokyo(7)
